(6534515, 970521, 107410, '<td valign="top">I am SO happy to see this ribbon! Stunning subject, perfect processing, the work of a pro!<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Beni Rodriguez:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by tanguera:</b></div><hr/><i> Love it when an image doesn''t use "expert" editing - and wins a ribbon.  Congrats. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>but... it should use it in an "expert editing" challenge!! ;) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Beni, you''re way off base here. The level of editing for a given challenge is merely the amount of processing that a photographer MAY use. Note in the rules of each challenge it says "May" or "May not," not Must or Must not. Deducting points (or not giving points...semantics) because someone was able to do more with less is rather trite.</td>', 'I am SO happy to see this ribbon! Stunning subject, perfect processing, the work of a pro! Originally posted by Beni Rodriguez: Originally posted by tanguera: Love it when an image doesn''t use "expert" editing - and wins a ribbon.  Congrats.   but... it should use it in an "expert editing" challenge!! ;)   Beni, you''re way off base here. The level of editing for a given challenge is merely the amount of processing that a photographer MAY use. Note in the rules of each challenge it says "May" or "May not," not Must or Must not. Deducting points (or not giving points...semantics) because someone was able to do more with less is rather trite.', '2011-09-09 03:26:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(6534527, 970285, 117474, '<td valign="top">Awesome :-)<br/>Great finish.</td>', 'Awesome :-)Great finish.', '2011-09-09 03:41:50.000000', NULL, 0),
